(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-14-knowledge-management-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 14: Knowledge Management Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"You are finishing residency and will begin practice. You want to establish a plan for keeping up with new developments in your field even though there are few professional colleagues in your community. All of the following might be useful, but which will be most useful to you?","choices":["Subscribe to a few good journals.","Subscribe to a service that reviews the literature in your field.","Buy new editions of printed textbooks.","Search MEDLINE at regular intervals.","Keep up contacts with colleagues in your training program by e-mail and telephone."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is virtually impossible to keep up with all scientifically strong, clinically relevant new research in your field without help from a publication that does that for you. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.1"},{"q":"You can rely on the best general medical journals in your field to:","choices":["Provide answers to clinical questions.","Assure that you have kept up with the medical literature.","Guarantee that the information they contain is beyond reproach.","Expose you to the many dimensions of your profession."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Journals are a rich source if information on the many aspects of being a physician—the history, politics, science, ideas, experiences, and more—but are not particularly good for finding answers to immediate questions or even surveillance on new developments in your field. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.2"},{"q":"Many children in your practice have attacks of otitis media. You want to base your management on the best available evidence. Which of the following is the least credible source of information on this question?","choices":["A clinical practice guideline by a major medical society","A systematic review published in a major journal","The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","The most recent research article on this question"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The resources listed in A–C are all excellent for looking up the answers to clinical questions, whereas a recent article, out of context with others of the same question, is of marginal value unless it happens to be much stronger than all the others that preceded it. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.3"},{"q":"A search of MEDLINE is especially useful for which of the following?","choices":["Finding all of the best articles bearing on a clinical question","An efficient strategy for finding the good articles","Looking for reports of rare events","Keeping up with the medical literature","Being familiar with the medical profession as a whole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. PubMed searches are indispensable for finding whether a rare event has been reported. They are one important part of an effort to find all published articles on a specific clinical question and are useful for researchers, but they are too inefficient for most questions at the point of care. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is accomplished by peer review of research manuscripts before they are published?","choices":["Exclude articles by authors with a conflict of interest.","Make the published article accurate and trustworthy.","Relieve readers of the need to be skeptical about the study.","Decide for the editors about whether they should publish the manuscript."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Although peer review and editing make articles better (more readable, accurate, and complete), articles are far from perfect when the process is over and they are published. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.5"},{"q":"An author of an article showing that screening colonoscopy is more effective in preventing colorectal cancer than other forms of screening would have conflicts of interest if he or she had any of the following except:","choices":["Clinical income from performing colonoscopies","Investment in a company that makes colonoscopies","Investment in medical products in general","Publications of articles that have consistently advocated colonoscopy as the best screening test","Rivalry with other scholars who advocate another screening test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Conflict of interest is in relation to a specific activity and does not exist in the general case of investing in medical products not specifically related to colonoscopy. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is the least useful way of looking up answers to clinical questions at the point of care?","choices":["Subscribing to several journals and keeping them available where you see patients","Guidelines on http://www.guidelines.gov","The Cochrane Library on the Internet","A continually updated electronic textbook"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. B–E are all valuable resources for looking up the answers to clinical questions at the point of care (if computers and the relevant programs are available). Medical journals have great value for other reasons, but they are not useful for this purpose. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.7"},{"q":"Which of the following should be least reassuring to a patient about the quality of a web site providing information about HIV?","choices":["The site is sponsored by a governmental agency and names its advisory board members.","The site provides facts, not opinions.","The primary source of information is stated.","The date of the last revision is posted and recent.","The author is a well-known expert in the field."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Patients and clinicians alike might well respect famous experts but should look for more solid footing—the organization that sponsors them, facts, and the source of those facts—when deciding whether to believe them. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is not part of grading clinical recommendations using the GRADE system?","choices":["Deciding whether to use a diagnostic test","Takes into account the balance of benefits and harms","Rates the quality of scientific evidence separately","Suggests how commonly and how forcefully a treatment should be recommended","Rates the strength of the evidence and of recommendations separately"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. GRADE provides a transparent method of grading the certainty of evidence, but by necessity relies on subjective judgment in the decisions. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.9"},{"q":"A comprehensive approach to managing knowledge in your field would include which of the following?","choices":["Subscribing to some journals and browsing them","Establishing a plan for looking up information at the point of care","Finding a publication that helps you keep up with new developments in your field","Identifying web sites you can recommend to your patients","All of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. All are basic elements of a comprehensive knowledge management plan, as described in this chapter. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 14.10"}]};
